+I run data organizations as a portfolio to be actively prioritized, not a set of pipelines to be kept alive. At SK Life Science I built and governed HCP/HCO, product, vendor, and commercial data domains supporting the company's first U.S. launch and its scale to $1 billion in annual commercial operations, on a Snowflake platform organized into bronze/silver/gold layers. Governance sat with a council of real business owners - Finance, Legal, HR, Quality - chaired by the COO, because compliance and data quality standards that live only inside IT rarely survive contact with a launch deadline. I have also put GenAI into production rather than just talked about it: agent-based automation of first-line security operations, an MSL next-best-action model built on Snowflake Cortex, and governed retrieval pipelines for internal knowledge, each with human review and validated systems of record as the source of truth. Cost discipline matters as much as delivery - I cut technology operating expense 30% through vendor renegotiation and cloud rightsizing while holding SLA attainment at 100%. I lead through empowered managers rather than a wide span of direct reports, which frees me to sit with commercial and functional leaders and argue priorities in business terms. That is the muscle I would bring to Merck's US HH domains - performance management, marketing mix, patient and HCP analytics - balancing what the business needs this quarter against the reliability and reuse a domain has to earn over years.